      "Record the person who can supply the missing fact and the person who can make the decision. Those may be different people, especially when a vendor and an internal owner are involved. Name both with a due date. A case with no fact owner will wait until someone notices it again.",
      "Add the amount and the customer so the log can be sorted by risk. A small exception can wait, while a payment or a customer promise may need same-day attention. Do not use the amount as the only priority signal. The consequence of the delay matters more than the size of the transaction.",
      "Record the date the missing fact was requested and the date it arrived. The gap between the two is part of the exception age. A fact that is requested but never supplied needs a different escalation from one that arrived late. The dates make that difference visible.",
      "## Give each exception one owner",
      "One person should own the exception until it closes. The owner may change at a shift handoff, but the log should show the change and the next action. A shared inbox is not an owner. An entry with three names is usually an entry with no owner.",
      "The owner should be able to state the next step without reading the whole thread. A short note in the log is enough if it names the action and the date. If the owner needs more context, link the record rather than pasting the conversation. A clean log is easier to hand off.",
      "Review the owner load during the weekly meeting. One specialist with twenty exceptions may be hiding a rule that does not work for a whole work type. Rebalance the cases or fix the rule before adding more reminders. The log should reduce repeat work, not just record it.",
      "Check that the owner is available during the period the decision is needed. A name on a log is not coverage if the person is on leave. Add a backup for the owner when the decision is time-sensitive. A short availability note prevents a stalled case.",
      "## Review the log for rule problems",
      "At the end of each week, group the exceptions by rule and by cause. A rule that appears often may be unclear rather than rare. A rule that appears once may be a genuine edge case. The pattern tells the owner whether to rewrite the guidance or add a new decision path.",
      "Do not treat every exception as a training failure. A specialist who follows an unclear rule is not the problem. Ask whether the rule can be stated in one sentence and whether the form asks for the facts it needs. A small change to the form can remove a recurring exception.",
      "Publish the change and the date it takes effect. Tell the team which cases should now follow the normal path. Keep the old entries for reference but mark the rule version. A log that shows the change is more useful than a log that only shows the backlog.",
      "Keep a list of the rules that produced the most exceptions in the last quarter. A rule that appears repeatedly is a candidate for rewriting. A rule that appears once may be a genuine edge case. Use the list to choose one rule to fix each month.",
      "## Close the exception with the outcome",
      "When the decision is made, record the outcome and the reason. A conditional approval should show the condition and who will check it. A denial should show the rule that supported it. The outcome is what turns one case into a reusable precedent.",
      "Tell the customer or the vendor what was decided, even when the answer is no. A clear message closes the loop and prevents a repeat call. Store the message with the exception entry. The next owner should be able to see what the other side was told. Record the delivery time as well so the message can be traced.",
      "Review closed exceptions monthly for cases that were reopened. A reopened case may mean the outcome was not communicated or the underlying rule is still unclear. Fix the cause before the next review. A short closure note is cheaper than a second investigation.",
