      "Break the movement down by work type and by state. A lane can look stable while one work type grows and another shrinks. Report the counts with the denominator so the reader can judge the change. A percentage without the base is not actionable.",
      "Highlight the oldest open item and the reason it is old. One example is easier to discuss than a long list. If the same reason appears in several items, name the pattern. The owner can then decide whether to change the rule or the staffing.",
      "Show the movement in a simple table with this week, last week, and the change. A small table is easier to scan than a paragraph. Put the work type with the largest change first. The owner can then ask about the exception rather than the routine.",
      "## Keep exceptions separate from routine work",
      "Exceptions deserve their own short section because they carry the most risk. Show the open exceptions, the owner, and the decision needed. Do not mix them with routine volume. A reader who has to hunt for the risky item will miss it.",
      "Report the exceptions that closed as well as the ones that remain. A closed exception shows that the process is working. A reopened exception shows that the outcome did not hold. Both are useful signals for the owner.",
      "Use the same categories each week so the report can be compared. If a new category appears, explain it in one line. A changing taxonomy makes the trend unreadable. Keep the list short and stable.",
      "Name the owner of each open exception and the date it was opened. An exception without a date looks new even when it is old. A date helps the owner judge the risk. A long list without dates is hard to prioritize.",
      "## Put the numbers in a small table",
